# Explore the Constraint Geometry of Sudoku

Generalized Sudoku is best understood as a graph-coloring problem. Begin with the exact construction, then inspect the graph obstruction, and finally compare random-instance models.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The formula is not merely a pattern generator: row, column, and block injectivity prove that it is a solution for every positive block size. Run the reproducible calculation below.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the arithmetic argument</summary>
Write a row index as $r=nq+s$. The row shift is $ns+q$, which swaps the two base-$n$ digits. This makes shifts distinct down columns. Within a block, $n(r mod n)+(c mod n)$ uniquely encodes the two within-block coordinates.
</details>

## The graph lower bound

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

The row clique supplies the lower bound $\chi\ge n^2$; the formula supplies the upper bound $\chi\le n^2$.

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact theorem</summary>
For every $n\ge1$, the Sudoku constraint graph has chromatic number exactly $n^2$. A filling is valid if and only if it is a proper coloring of this graph.
</details>

## Density is not an ensemble

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

Every restriction of a completed grid remains solvable. Therefore completion-restriction sampling has solvability probability one at every density.

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the monotonicity principle</summary>
If a clue system with more clues is solvable, deleting clues preserves its witness. Adding clues can only remove solutions. This does not create a universal threshold because the values and their correlations depend on the sampling law.
</details>

## Reproduce and extend

{{algorithm:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:2}}

Use these components as controls when testing a named random-value ensemble. For background, see [graph coloring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_coloring), [constraint satisfaction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constraint_satisfaction_problem), and [phase transitions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_transition). Keep existence, uniqueness, and solver cost as separate observables.

